{
  "id": "Compound::DB00572 - Gene::1131",
  "verbalization": "Atropine, a well-known cholinergic muscarinic antagonist, is linked to Chrm3 through a shared biological pathway involving antimuscarinic agents. The evidence suggests that Atropine, as part of the broader class of cholinergic muscarinic antagonists, binds to and inhibits the muscarinic receptor Chrm3. This is supported by the fact that multiple compounds within the same class—Darifenacin, Tolterodine, and Oxybutynin—bind to Chrm3, indicating a common mechanism of action. The lowest common ancestors of these compounds, Atropine and the respective drugs, point to a shared pharmacological profile, reinforcing the idea that Atropine exerts its effects through a mechanism that involves interaction with muscarinic receptors, specifically Chrm3. The integration of these pathways highlights the role of antimuscarinic agents in modulating muscarinic signaling, with Atropine serving as a representative member of this class that directly interacts with Chrm3.",
